WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:04.640
Diandra Leslie-Pelecky: I
thought that was really creative

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.140
and totally possible.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.990
My name is Diandra Leslie-Pelecky.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:10.520
I have a PhD in physics,
and I started off my life

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:13.640
as a condensed-matter physicist
working in nanomedicine.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.557
Along the way, I ended
up writing a book called

00:00:15.557 --> 00:00:16.727
"The Physics of NASCAR."

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:18.551
So today I'm gonna use that knowledge

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.092
to talk to you about some clips

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:21.462
from "Fast and Furious."

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.670
This is one that is a
little difficult to analyze,

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:43.660
because you're not quite sure
how long they're falling.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.880
It could be anywhere from 11
seconds to maybe 15 seconds.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:50.360
That's a really long time to fall.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:51.993
I calculated out

00:00:51.993 --> 00:00:54.576
what Dom's terminal velocity would be,

00:00:54.576 --> 00:00:56.320
and that's biggest velocity you can fall

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.710
given that drag gets bigger
as the speed goes up.

00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:01.350
And he almost approaches it.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:02.791
After 11 seconds,

00:01:02.791 --> 00:01:06.041
Dom would be going 124 miles per hour

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:07.265
when he hit the water.

00:01:07.265 --> 00:01:11.898
He would have fallen about 1,775 feet

00:01:11.898 --> 00:01:13.339
at that point.

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:16.939
Now, 124 miles per hour into water,

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:18.470
maybe that's survivable.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:22.070
Laso Schaller holds the
record for the highest jump.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.310
That was from 193 feet.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.980
He hit the water at 76 miles an hour.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.280
He broke his right clavicle
and dislocated his pelvis.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.300
The Golden Gate Bridge is about 245 feet.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.850
People hit the water at 86 miles an hour.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.060
Only 5% of the people who jump survive.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.670
So the probability of them
surviving is pretty small.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:46.377
They built a launcher
and took this beautiful

00:01:46.377 --> 00:01:49.879
1966 Corvette and actually launched it

00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:51.062
off the side of the cliff,

00:01:51.062 --> 00:01:52.470
and then they had stuntmen

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.050
who did it separately from the car

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.433
because being hit by a car midair

00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:59.050
would be worse than
actually hitting the water.

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.430
And so they had the stuntmen do it,

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.020
but the stuntmen were on cables.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.260
Then they came back,
did it on a green screen

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.470
with the actual actors,
who were, you know,

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.077
maybe eight feet above the ground.

00:02:08.077 --> 00:02:11.070
But this one I think I
would have to give a score

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.750
of two, because there's absolutely no way

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.241
they could've survived a jump that long.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.920
So, this a 1969 Camaro.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:29.760
It's got a mass of about 3,500 pounds.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:30.955
So, they're hitting 90, so you know

00:02:30.955 --> 00:02:33.278
the boat's going at
least 90 miles an hour.

00:02:33.278 --> 00:02:35.290
One of the things that you notice is that

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.730
when the car and the boat
are both speeding along,

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:39.320
they're parallel to each other.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.330
Now, how they all of a
sudden get behind the boat,

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.670
I'm not actually sure how that happens.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.700
But let's assume they can do that.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.708
When they take off, they're
going at approximately

00:02:48.708 --> 00:02:52.070
45-degree angle off this ramp,

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:54.100
and they're going 120 miles an hour.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.700
So not all of the speed

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:57.850
is taking them in a horizontal direction.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.380
So I've taken cosine of
45; they're only now going

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.440
about 78 miles per hour toward the boat.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.981
Now, that's a problem,

00:03:04.981 --> 00:03:07.170
because the boat and the car looked like

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:08.710
they were going the same speed.

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.917
And the problem is, if the
boat's going 100 miles per hour,

00:03:11.917 --> 00:03:13.967
it'll be 1,200 feet from the shore.

00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:17.240
The problem is, the car
can only go 945 feet

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.580
in the time it's in the air.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.800
There's actually no way

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.890
that the car could make it onto the boat.

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:24.840
Let's pretend the boat is
only going 78 miles an hour,

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.290
because if they are,
it's totally possible.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.078
It is going fast enough, and it makes up

00:03:29.078 --> 00:03:31.600
all the distance between it and the boat.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.355
So if you include drag,

00:03:33.355 --> 00:03:35.040
they're gonna lose some of their speed

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.360
due to air resistance.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:37.700
They're still gonna hit that boat

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.210
at around 105 miles per hour.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.930
The kinetic energy of the car

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:44.050
and the guys in the car, at that point,

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.390
is equal to about a
fifth of a pound of TNT.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:49.020
Some of that goes into wrecking the boat

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.270
and wrecking the car, but not all of it.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.400
Which means some of it is
gonna go into the guys,

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.550
and they're gonna have a whole lot worse

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:56.842
than a broken arm.

00:03:56.842 --> 00:03:59.062
I think I'd give it
probably a seven out of 10,

00:03:59.062 --> 00:04:01.848
because it's possible it could happen.

00:04:01.848 --> 00:04:03.373
They had to modify this tank

00:04:03.373 --> 00:04:04.681
so it could go 60 miles an hour,

00:04:04.681 --> 00:04:06.871
'cause they normally
only go 30 miles an hour.

00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.670
Dom, we'll assume his car
was going 60 miles an hour,

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.710
so when he left it, he was
going 60 miles an hour.

00:04:19.710 --> 00:04:21.810
So you can look at
conservation of momentum

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.350
and figure out that by the
time they hit and continued on,

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.000
they were probably going
around 40 miles an hour.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.039
So a little bit of change in speed,

00:04:30.039 --> 00:04:32.764
but the problem is when he hit the car

00:04:32.764 --> 00:04:36.930
going 40 miles an hour, and
she is about 120 pounds,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:41.910
he's about 225, so you're
talking about 340 or so pounds.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.620
You're gonna be hitting
it with an acceleration

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.810
of almost 100 g.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:46.090
They have no protective gear.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.450
So, you know, race-car drivers

00:04:47.450 --> 00:04:49.700
routinely survive hits of 100 g.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.920
They've got a big car around them.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.574
And maybe it's possible that
Dom could launch himself

00:04:54.574 --> 00:04:56.890
at just the right angle
to intercept Letty.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:58.690
And then maybe it's possible that

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.598
that path took you onto the second bridge

00:05:01.598 --> 00:05:04.349
instead of over the first one,

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:07.090
and maybe it's possible that
there happened to be a car

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:08.670
at the point where they were landing,

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:09.745
and maybe it's possible

00:05:09.745 --> 00:05:12.003
they survived hitting the car together.

00:05:12.003 --> 00:05:13.870
Each one of those things
might have happened,

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.306
but all four of them together

00:05:15.306 --> 00:05:17.065
is just too much of a coincidence.

00:05:17.065 --> 00:05:20.020
Once you're in the air, you cannot steer.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.578
So if you misjudge in any way,

00:05:22.578 --> 00:05:23.878
you're gonna miss her.

00:05:23.878 --> 00:05:25.970
Now, if you watch baseball players,

00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:28.630
they develop this very intuitive ability

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:30.480
to know the arc of a ball.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:32.710
And after all, it's
just a parabolic motion.

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.850
Maybe just Dom understands physics so well

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.960
that he knew exactly
where to launch himself.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.530
And if you talk to a race-car driver,

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:42.740
they would be able to understand
centripetal acceleration

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:44.720
and explain it to you.
Again, no equations,

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.310
but they get this intuitive
understanding for it.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:50.083
So, it's just possible, maybe Dom has

00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:52.410
rescued enough people who
are flying through the air

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.243
that he understands how to do it.

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:04.480
Hollywood has its own physics.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.110
One of the rules is
they have time dilation,

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:09.510
which is that when something
really exciting is happening,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.800
it can take as long as
you want it to take.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.890
Slowing down, let's say 120 miles an hour,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:16.910
and I pick that number only because

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.639
that'll tell you, at 120 miles an hour,

00:06:19.639 --> 00:06:22.742
the car is going two miles every minute.

00:06:22.742 --> 00:06:25.340
So for every minute of that clip,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:27.010
you're traveling two miles.

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:31.600
The longest airport runway
in the word is 3.4 miles,

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:33.310
which means if they were on that runway,

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:35.960
this still could take 1.7 minutes.

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:36.793
And it doesn't.

00:06:36.793 --> 00:06:39.730
It takes a lot, it takes,
like, 11 or 12 minutes,

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:43.933
which would result in just
this unfathomably long runway.

00:06:43.933 --> 00:06:47.490
OK, so Dom is driving
a 2012 Dodge Charger,

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:50.690
and let's pretend this is
a super-reinforced version

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:51.920
of a Dodge Charger.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.680
He doesn't have a lot
of time to get up speed.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.720
Because he's only got
the inside of a plane,

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.640
and as you saw from the
fight that was going on,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.080
there's not a lot of room.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.410
I think I have to give a one.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:03.500
Just because here,

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.483
they stretched my
ability to ignore reality

00:07:06.483 --> 00:07:09.124
just a little further
than I'm capable of doing.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.120
Try to figure out how
much that vault weighs,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:16.600
according to the storyline.

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:18.940
So, we're gonna assume it's a 5-ton vault.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.883
That's about 10,000 pounds.

00:07:20.883 --> 00:07:24.360
There's $100 million in $100 bills.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.586
That's gonna give us another 2,200 pounds.

00:07:27.586 --> 00:07:30.369
Dom is 225 pounds,

00:07:30.369 --> 00:07:34.310
and the car is 4,180 pounds.

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:39.305
That gives us a total
weight of 16,605 pounds

00:07:39.305 --> 00:07:41.740
that his car has to accelerate.

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:44.220
If you ever tried to move something heavy,

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:46.552
like a refrigerator or a bookcase,

00:07:46.552 --> 00:07:48.190
you push on it, and it doesn't move.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.123
And you push harder, it doesn't move.

00:07:50.123 --> 00:07:52.149
And you push harder, and finally it moves.

00:07:52.149 --> 00:07:53.631
That moment when it moves,

00:07:53.631 --> 00:07:56.660
you've just overcome the
static frictional force.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.755
The static frictional force is higher

00:07:58.755 --> 00:08:00.469
than the kinetic frictional force.

00:08:00.469 --> 00:08:02.280
And what that means in real-people talk

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:04.110
is that once you get something moving,

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.040
it's much easier to keep it moving.

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:07.250
So the question is,

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.390
is it possible for this 2010 Charger SRT

00:08:11.390 --> 00:08:15.419
to overcome the friction needed to move?

00:08:15.419 --> 00:08:17.710
So we're gonna calculate
how long it would take him

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.649
to accelerate up to 50 miles an hour.

00:08:20.649 --> 00:08:23.400
We've got the 425-horsepower car

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:27.170
accelerating 16,605 pounds.

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:30.230
And it turns out that would
take about 5.86 seconds,

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.460
which is a little longer than it took him

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.430
to actually do it in the movie.

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:38.000
So the fact that he got the
car accelerating that fast,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.940
that definitely couldn't
happen in real life.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:41.050
But that's without friction.

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:42.730
So once you put friction in,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.380
what you find if you calculate

00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:47.500
is that the coefficient
of friction between, like,

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.870
steel and asphalt is pretty high.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.844
And you probably could not,

00:08:52.844 --> 00:08:55.044
even with a nitrous oxide boost,

00:08:55.044 --> 00:08:57.100
get the vault moving.

00:08:57.100 --> 00:08:59.450
So, once you got it moving,
you could keep it moving.

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.335
What the folks have
said who did this stunt,

00:09:02.335 --> 00:09:03.400
they found the same thing.

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:06.240
They learned a lot about
static friction the hard way.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.590
And they actually put a slippery material

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.210
on the bottom of the
vault to get it moving.

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.870
But one of the problems with that

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:15.291
is when you make something
easier to start moving,

00:09:15.291 --> 00:09:17.550
you also make it easier to stop moving.

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.150
And my understanding is
they were very surprised

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.080
when they thought it would come to a stop

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:24.700
and it took a lot longer to
stop than they thought it would.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.080
The other thing is the question of,

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.010
you have this fairly small
mass of a car and Dom.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.010
That's about 2,000 pounds.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.535
And the mass of the vault,

00:09:33.535 --> 00:09:35.770
which is about 15,000 pounds.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:39.326
So you've got something
that's seven times bigger

00:09:39.326 --> 00:09:40.510
than something else.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:43.090
Now, think about when they're coming down

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:45.630
and then Dom starts, stops,
and he lets the vault

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:46.870
sort of swing around.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:49.610
The vault is, you know,
seven times heavier

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:50.800
than Dom and his car.

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:54.301
And I just don't buy that
he has enough traction

00:09:54.301 --> 00:09:57.760
to swing the vault without the
vault pulling his car along.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:10:00.280
Making these stunts work in real life,

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.721
with practical effects and not CGI,

00:10:02.721 --> 00:10:05.700
so they built something like
seven or eight different vaults

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.050
with different weights
so that they could do

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.613
all the different parts with the vault.

00:10:09.613 --> 00:10:12.430
And in fact, in some
points, they actually had

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:14.990
a little semi cab inside the vault

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.180
and the vault was
driving around by itself.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.904
So I would give this stunt a five.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.980
I mean, we've all tried to go
up a down escalator, right?

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.263
That's all he's doing there.

00:10:35.782 --> 00:10:37.700
This whole stunt relies on

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.575
the person being able to run faster

00:10:39.575 --> 00:10:40.883
than the bus is falling.

00:10:40.883 --> 00:10:43.440
When they filmed this, the
stuntperson did actually

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:45.170
run up the bus as it was falling.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:46.910
I had some real questions, because

00:10:46.910 --> 00:10:49.440
it's not like you're
pushing off something solid.

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:50.830
You're pushing off something that's moving

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:52.200
in the other direction.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:55.050
It spun around to be in
exactly the right position

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.240
for him to grab the bar.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.480
When he's holding the bar
and she stops suddenly,

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.912
you get this really great example of

00:11:00.912 --> 00:11:02.120
Newton's law of motion

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.600
where an object in
motion will keep moving,

00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.460
because she stops the car, and
he keeps going flying around.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.280
I would give this one a
10 for running up the bus.

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:13.547
I thought that was really
creative and totally possible.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.270
Again, you see a lot of tight shots,

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:27.712
so it does make it hard to see

00:11:27.712 --> 00:11:29.760
what's going on necessarily.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:31.785
This is a Lykan hypercar,

00:11:31.785 --> 00:11:33.993
and it's got 780 horsepower.

00:11:33.993 --> 00:11:36.680
This car is $3.4 million.

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:38.520
There's only seven of them in the world.

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.650
Sometimes I think they
put it in slow motion

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.823
just so people like me

00:11:41.823 --> 00:11:44.830
can't do the detailed
calculations and mess with them.

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.890
How fast do you have to be going

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:50.140
so that you can span the
distance between the buildings?

00:11:50.140 --> 00:11:53.770
So, if we assume that
distance is 150 feet,

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:54.910
you're gonna fall a little bit.

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:58.000
So anytime something
comes off horizontally,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.310
it's going to go down a little bit.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.160
And you can actually see it in there,

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.440
that it probably falls about two stories,

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.740
which is roughly 20 feet.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.303
And so the question is,

00:12:07.303 --> 00:12:10.760
how fast do you have to be
going to fall two stories

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.470
in 150 feet of horizontal distance?

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.770
If you're gonna fall two floors,

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:16.930
it would take just a
little more than a second,

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.920
and you'd need to be
going 137 miles per hour.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.370
If you fall four floors, it
would be about 1.6 seconds,

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:25.690
and you'd have to be going
only 95 miles per hour.

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:29.790
Now, this car has a top
speed of 245 miles per hour.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.470
Top speed is no problem.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:32.980
What is a problem, however,

00:12:32.980 --> 00:12:35.520
is how long it takes
you to get to top speed.

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:40.507
So, the car goes 0 to 60
miles an hour in 2.8 seconds.

00:12:40.507 --> 00:12:43.475
In order to do that, it needs 123 feet

00:12:43.475 --> 00:12:45.400
if you assume constant acceleration.

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.488
Those look like pretty small spaces,

00:12:47.488 --> 00:12:49.592
because he was doing
an awful lot of turning

00:12:49.592 --> 00:12:50.980
as he was going around.

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.590
And that's only 60 miles an hour.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.548
If you wanted to get to 125 miles an hour,

00:12:55.548 --> 00:12:56.598
according to the specs,

00:12:56.598 --> 00:12:59.240
that would take you 9.4 seconds.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:03.260
You would need 861 feet
to reach that speed,

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.926
and I don't see how you'd do that

00:13:04.926 --> 00:13:06.770
in that tiny little tower.

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:08.426
And the other problem is that

00:13:08.426 --> 00:13:10.010
when they're just driving around,

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:12.620
they must be pulling tremendous g-forces,

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.020
because they're driving
these tiny little circles.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.210
I tried to look up the
coefficient of friction

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:19.615
between tires and marble, but it turns out

00:13:19.615 --> 00:13:21.580
that it's very hard to
find because most people

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:24.940
do not drive cars in
their apartment buildings.

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:27.730
So I couldn't find that, but even so,

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:30.630
you know, I question, at
going at high speed on marble,

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:33.410
whether he could even keep
traction going around like that.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.623
You know, again, you've
got another example of,

00:13:35.623 --> 00:13:37.969
there's a bunch of
things that could happen,

00:13:37.969 --> 00:13:41.040
and if you put them all
together, you might not buy it.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:44.010
Even the very strong, reinforced glass

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.523
they would use on a skyscraper like this

00:13:46.523 --> 00:13:50.155
is not gonna stand up to
a car that's 3,000 pounds

00:13:50.155 --> 00:13:53.750
traveling at 120, 130 miles per hour.

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:56.534
The kinetic energy of
the car is just too high.

00:14:03.421 --> 00:14:05.571
Was he not wearing a
seatbelt the whole time?

00:14:07.792 --> 00:14:10.425
I just love the look after
he does these things.

00:14:10.425 --> 00:14:12.350
I mean, he just sort of looks like, "Oh."

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.140
I understand this Charger
was especially modified

00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:16.610
so it could drive on ice.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:19.660
Obviously, the ice here
is gonna be pretty thick,

00:14:19.660 --> 00:14:21.360
but if you've got that much heat,

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:22.410
it's gonna melt the ice,

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:24.350
and that's gonna make it
much harder to get traction.

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:26.963
The physics of how the car
would move after he hit it,

00:14:26.963 --> 00:14:28.379
that was pretty accurate.

00:14:28.379 --> 00:14:29.860
I was pretty impressed by that.

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:30.977
One of the things that happens

00:14:30.977 --> 00:14:32.268
that people don't appreciate

00:14:32.268 --> 00:14:34.040
is when a car is on fire,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.600
the fire quickly depletes all the oxygen.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:37.760
And, you know, it's using it up,

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:40.540
and if you're in a car,
you get very, very warm,

00:14:40.540 --> 00:14:42.220
you get disoriented, there's smoke,

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:43.410
you can't see what you're doing.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.830
And so I think, when something's on fire,

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:47.340
him coming out of this amount of fire,

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.420
that's got some issues
with the realism, too.

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.662
I don't see any reason
why this couldn't happen.

00:14:51.662 --> 00:14:53.475
So this one I would have to give a 10.

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:55.025
I think this one's pretty plausible.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:06.370
So, if you look up a
fuel tanker that holds,

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:09.570
oh, I don't know, maybe
7,000 gallons of gasoline,

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:13.940
the density of gasoline is
6.073 pounds per gallon,

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:17.640
about 42,000 pounds coming toward them.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.700
Now, if it were something doing, you know,

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:22.270
moving regularly, something bouncing,

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:24.130
that'd be fine because you could gauge it.

00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:26.410
It's like a jump rope, where
you know it's coming around,

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:27.550
you know to jump.

00:15:27.550 --> 00:15:28.470
But the problem with this

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:29.870
is if you look how it's rolling,

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:33.170
it's rolling in this direction
and it's rolling forward.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:35.200
So there's no way you can predict

00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:37.240
exactly how it's going to roll.

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.990
And if the tanker is 42,000 pounds

00:15:39.990 --> 00:15:41.830
and the car is 4,000 pounds,

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:43.470
it's gonna squish the car.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:46.900
So, my first car was a 1988 Buick LeSabre,

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.710
which is about the same size

00:15:48.710 --> 00:15:52.390
as the 1987 Buick Grand
National that you see.

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:54.530
The chances of it rolling over the tanker,

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:55.670
pretty darn slow.

00:15:55.670 --> 00:15:56.747
When the tanker starts rolling,

00:15:56.747 --> 00:15:59.030
it's got a certain
amount of kinetic energy,

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:01.010
but every time it hits something,

00:16:01.010 --> 00:16:02.914
like the wall of the canyon or the ground,

00:16:02.914 --> 00:16:05.190
it's gonna lose some
of that kinetic energy.

00:16:05.190 --> 00:16:07.270
And that means it's not
gonna bounce as high.

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:09.210
So actually the longer he waits,

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.900
the harder it is gonna be for him

00:16:10.900 --> 00:16:13.020
to get his car under that tanker.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:14.528
So I would give that one a three.

00:16:25.694 --> 00:16:28.490
So, this a Nissan 350Z, I believe.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.707
The person inside the
car is actually using

00:16:31.707 --> 00:16:33.530
a combination of the steering wheel,

00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:34.871
the clutch, and the brakes

00:16:34.871 --> 00:16:37.190
to make the car go around the turn.

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:38.965
And it turns out it's actually easier

00:16:38.965 --> 00:16:40.690
to drift around the turn

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:42.550
than it is to drift in
a straight direction.

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:45.150
I believe that this guy
could probably do that,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.092
but I'd like to know
how many takes it took.

00:16:47.092 --> 00:16:49.870
But you can drift for as long
as your tires will hold out.

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:51.545
It's a problem because you want to have

00:16:51.545 --> 00:16:54.272
equal traction on all four wheels.

00:16:54.272 --> 00:16:56.060
And that means you need
the same amount of weight.

00:16:56.060 --> 00:16:57.710
When drifting, what you want is

00:16:57.710 --> 00:16:59.043
you want the rear end of the car

00:16:59.043 --> 00:17:00.709
to be able to slide back and forth.

00:17:00.709 --> 00:17:03.680
When you're actually driving
where the tires are rolling,

00:17:03.680 --> 00:17:05.850
you're actually sliding those tires.

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:07.730
And so it's a totally
different phenomenon.

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:10.660
At some point, you're going
to do enough damage to them

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:11.980
that they're probably going to pop.

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:14.105
A good driver can definitely do that.

00:17:14.105 --> 00:17:16.570
Now, if they would've shown the other guy,

00:17:16.570 --> 00:17:18.250
who's just learning, doing that,

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:19.742
I would've given it a zero.

00:17:26.853 --> 00:17:29.720
This is a perfect example
of a static equilibrium.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:31.603
So, as long as all the cars

00:17:31.603 --> 00:17:33.594
are pulling with the same force,

00:17:33.594 --> 00:17:34.820
Dom can't go anywhere.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:35.920
And what that means is,

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:38.080
imagine it's just Dom and two cars,

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.100
one pulling left, one pulling right.

00:17:40.100 --> 00:17:42.647
Those two cars are
pulling with equal force.

00:17:42.647 --> 00:17:44.040
Dom can't do anything.

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:44.873
The problem you have is

00:17:44.873 --> 00:17:48.222
the traction between
the tires and the ground

00:17:48.222 --> 00:17:50.100
is what is our limiting factor here.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.158
And they're talking about

00:17:51.158 --> 00:17:53.420
his car having 2,000 horsepower,

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:55.819
3,000 horsepower, 5,000 horsepower.

00:17:55.819 --> 00:17:57.270
I don't know if it has that much.

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:58.540
All it has to have

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:01.110
is enough horsepower to
get a little bit of slack.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:02.650
What he's doing, is all he has to do

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:05.040
is get enough slack that
he can move forward,

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.085
but what I'm not understanding here is

00:18:07.085 --> 00:18:09.340
what's happening to the
forces on him from behind.

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.430
And, of course, once he gets one of them,

00:18:11.430 --> 00:18:13.568
then you've got unbalanced force.

00:18:13.568 --> 00:18:14.690
And the only reason this works

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:18.226
is because the net sum of
all the forces on this car

00:18:18.226 --> 00:18:19.730
is gonna be zero.

00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:22.650
And once he breaks free of
one, all hell breaks loose.

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:26.092
This only works if the
people on the other side

00:18:26.092 --> 00:18:27.510
aren't doing their job right.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:29.760
We have a theory that one of
the people in the other cars

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:31.450
momentarily lost concentration

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:33.310
and that must be how he got out of it.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:34.419
Because that's all we could figure out

00:18:34.419 --> 00:18:35.565
how to make it happen.

00:18:35.565 --> 00:18:38.092
And the problem giving
something like this a grade

00:18:38.092 --> 00:18:40.380
is that you don't get to see all of it,

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.863
because you're seeing the
insides of each of the cars,

00:18:42.863 --> 00:18:44.440
and you're not seeing the overhead view.

00:18:44.440 --> 00:18:46.740
It's really hard to see how that happened.

00:19:00.225 --> 00:19:02.584
The doctor has to have
taken that into account,

00:19:02.584 --> 00:19:06.161
because the purpose of the
cast is to immobilize the arm,

00:19:06.161 --> 00:19:07.465
and you have to take into account

00:19:07.465 --> 00:19:10.029
the fact that he could flex his arm, so.

00:19:10.029 --> 00:19:13.365
Could flexing the arm
break the plaster of paris?

00:19:13.365 --> 00:19:14.380
I bet it could.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:15.521
But wouldn't you assume, I mean,

00:19:15.521 --> 00:19:19.143
maybe that's why he's always
wearing sleeveless clothing.

00:19:19.143 --> 00:19:20.630
You know, it's hard to extrapolate,

00:19:20.630 --> 00:19:23.260
because I know I couldn't do that.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:24.880
So you watch some of those things,

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:28.170
and you go, "Yeah, he
might be able to do that."

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.450
They aren't exactly 100% true to life.

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:33.050
I love these movies. I
think they're fun to watch.

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:35.610
And I think it's really
great to watch something

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.540
knowing the good guys
are gonna win in the end.

